---
/**
 * MapVisitBlock — the `map_visit` layout: the "visit us" band. A copy column
 * with the practice's address card beside an embedded Google Map.
 *
 * EXTRACTED, NOT DESIGNED. Two live pages ship this shape and this component is
 * their union, element for element:
 *
 *   src/pages/contact/index.astro:183-252     `#visit`
 *   src/pages/our-office/index.astro:238-304  `#visit`
 *
 * `.location-grid` / `.location-copy` / `.location-card` / `.hours-table` /
 * `.location-map-wrap` are the pages' own class names on purpose, the same
 * reason FaqBlock keeps `.faq-list`: both page stylesheets still carry rules for
 * them and keep carrying them until the stylesheet pass removes their copies, so
 * renaming anything here to `vs-map-visit__*` would unstyle the band the moment
 * an editor's backfill flipped `blocks` from empty to populated — on a live
 * client page, between deploys, with nobody watching.
 *
 * FOUR COPY FIELDS AND NOTHING ELSE, BECAUSE EVERYTHING ELSE IS SITE DATA.
 * Street, city, state, zip, phone, email, opening hours, both buttons and the
 * map embed all resolve from src/data/contact.ts and src/data/hours.ts inside
 * this file — the same rule AreaBand and LocalTrust already follow, and the
 * reason this layout has no cta fields at all. A `tel:` URL or a maps URL in a
 * CMS field is a phone number with a second home, and the second one goes stale.
 *
 * THE ADDRESS LINE IS ONE EXPRESSION. `${addressCity}, ${addressState}
 * ${addressZip}` is built in the frontmatter and printed once. Written the
 * template's way — `{addressCity}, {addressState} {addressZip}` — the single
 * space between the last two expressions is a whitespace TEXT NODE, and that is
 * how "Parker, CO 80134" shipped as "Parker, CO80134" on three pages of this
 * project. It survives on these two today (verified in
 * dist/contact/index.html and dist/our-office/index.html), because the
 * compiler keeps a same-line space between two expressions and drops it only
 * across a line break. Same output either way; one expression cannot regress.
 *
 * `ariaLabel` IS A FIELD BECAUSE THE TWO PAGES DISAGREE — "Visit our practice"
 * against "Visit our office". Blank emits NO attribute rather than an empty
 * one: `aria-label=""` strips a landmark's accessible name instead of leaving
 * it to the heading.
 *
 * ── THE TWO SKINS, AND THE ONE THEY CONVERGE ON ──────────────────────────────
 *
 * This band exists twice on the live site with two different card treatments,
 * and a single component cannot ship both on the same band value:
 *
 *   contact.css:288-350   glass card — rgba(255,255,255,.06) ground,
 *                         translucent hairlines, #fff type. Written FOR the
 *                         charcoal band ("LOCATION + HOURS (charcoal-green)").
 *   our-office.css:341-400  white card — #fff ground, --line hairlines, --ink
 *                         type. Written for a CREAM band ("Mirrors /contact/
 *                         but on a cream band instead of sage dark"), and left
 *                         unchanged when the band itself was flipped to
 *                         charcoal. Only `.lede` was re-asserted for the flip
 *                         (:338-339) — the card was not.
 *
 * Both rows in the Wave-B contract are `band: charcoal`. So BOTH treatments are
 * carried below, keyed on this block's own band modifier: the dark bands (`alt`,
 * `sage`) get contact's glass card, the light bands (paper, cream, sage-pale)
 * get our-office's white one. That keeps each source treatment alive on the band
 * it was drawn for, and it means /our-office/ #visit changes from a white card
 * on charcoal to a glass card on charcoal. THAT IS A DELIBERATE, VISIBLE,
 * ONE-BAND CORRECTION — the same class of change as `Parker, CO80134` ->
 * `Parker, CO 80134` on the hub bands, and it must be recorded in the block-map,
 * not left for the diff to find. It is invisible to a word-level diff and
 * invisible to a section-class diff; only a screenshot shows it.
 *
 * The geometry below is contact.css's throughout, for the same reason: that
 * sheet was written for this band on THIS band colour. /our-office/'s numbers
 * differ in seven small places and each divergence is named at the rule that
 * supersedes it, so the stylesheet pass can see what it is deleting.
 *
 * WHAT /contact/ LOSES: one `light` class token on the eyebrow. The house rule
 * every other block follows is `light` on the sage-pale and sage bands only
 * (FaqBlock:139), and this block owns the charcoal eyebrow colour in its own
 * scoped CSS, so nothing moves visually. Recorded in manifest.ts:1067-1073.
 *
 * FIRST BLOCK TO SHIP FONTAWESOME MARKUP. The four `<i class="fa-solid …">`
 * glyphs are the component's, not fields — address, phone, email and hours are
 * a fixed four-row card, not a repeater. `fontawesome.css` + `solid.css` are
 * imported once in BaseLayout.astro:11-12, so they are on every route this
 * block can land on; nothing extra ships.
 *
 * NOTHING HERE THROWS. An editor triggers deploys and never sees the output
 * (cms/mu-plugins/vs-deploy.php:142-158), so every field is read as if it might
 * be missing, blank, or the wrong type.
 *
 * Styles are co-located per docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 2.5. Every rule below names the
 * sheet and line it was lifted from; the page sheets' local `--sage` / `--ink` /
 * `--line` / `--f-display` aliases are resolved back to the `--vs-*` tokens they
 * alias one-for-one (contact.css:14-36, our-office.css:20-40), so rendered
 * values are unchanged.
 *
 * Spec: docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md 1.2, 2.5, 3.3.
 */

import Button from "../components/Button.astro";
import {
  addressCity,
  addressLine,
  addressState,
  addressStreet,
  addressZip,
  emailAddress,
  emailHref,
  phoneHref,
  phoneLabel,
} from "../data/contact";
import { officeHours } from "../data/hours";
import { resolveCta } from "./cta";

/**
 * band value -> the modifier class this block writes on its own section.
 *
 * Read this map, never an ancestor class. `.section.alt` is charcoal green on
 * these two pages and cream on teeth-whitening.css:237 (Fact 4), so a block that
 * inherited its band would be wrong on one of them the moment a section moved.
 * `paper` maps to nothing at all because a paper band is a bare
 * `class="section"` on every page in the corpus — adding a modifier would be the
 * diff.
 */
const BAND_MODIFIER: Record<string, string | null> = {
  paper: null,
  cream: "cream",
  "sage-pale": "dark",
  sage: "sage",
  charcoal: "alt",
};

interface Props {
  /** DOM id, already deduplicated by PageBlocks.astro. Absent when blank. */
  id?: string;
  /** The same value as `id`. Accepted so the spread in PageBlocks is lossless. */
  anchor?: string | null;
  /** Rail label. Read by the page that builds the rail, not by this band. */
  navLabel?: string | null;
  band?: string | null;
  eyebrow?: string | null;
  /** May contain `<em>`, and nothing else (docs/PAGE-BLOCKS.md, Fact 3). */
  heading?: string | null;
  /** The `p.lede` under the heading. */
  body?: string | null;
  /**
   * The section's accessible name. A field because the two pages disagree.
   * Blank emits no attribute at all — see the header.
   */
  ariaLabel?: string | null;
}

const { id, band, eyebrow, heading, body, ariaLabel } = Astro.props;

/* `?? null` rather than a default: an unknown band from a future PHP choice
   falls through to the paper shell rather than emitting a class with no rule. */
const bandModifier = BAND_MODIFIER[band ?? "paper"] ?? null;
const bandKey = typeof band === "string" && band !== "" ? band : "paper";

/** Trim once, here, so every guard below tests for real content rather than for
 *  a field an editor left as a single space. */
const text = (input: unknown): string =>
  typeof input === "string" ? input.trim() : "";

const eyebrowText = text(eyebrow);
const headingHtml = text(heading);
const bodyText = text(body);
const ariaLabelText = text(ariaLabel);

/**
 * `light` on the two light-on-dark-ish bands only — the house rule FaqBlock:139
 * states and every block follows. /contact/ writes it on its charcoal band and
 * /our-office/ does not; the block picks the rule, not the page, and the
 * charcoal eyebrow colour is owned below either way.
 */
const eyebrowLight = bandKey === "sage-pale" || bandKey === "sage";

/** Solid button on a dark band has to be the light variant, not the sage one. */
const isDarkBand = bandKey === "charcoal" || bandKey === "sage";

/**
 * The two buttons resolve through the ONE shared token map and hover table in
 * ./cta.ts, even though neither is an editor field: `map` -> directionsHref +
 * "Open Map", `phone` -> phoneHref + "Tap to Dial", which is byte-for-byte what
 * both templates hardcode. Going through the resolver rather than importing
 * `directionsHref` and retyping the labels keeps this from becoming the sixth
 * copy of a table that gets edited — the reason cta.ts exists at all.
 */
const directionsCta = resolveCta("map", "");
const phoneCta = resolveCta("phone", "");

/**
 * City/state/zip as ONE expression. See the header: three spliced expressions
 * are how "Parker, CO80134" reached three live pages.
 */
const cityStateZip = `${addressCity}, ${addressState} ${addressZip}`;

/* `output=embed` avoids needing a Google Maps API key — contact/index.astro:31,
   our-office/index.astro:37, character for character. */
const mapEmbedSrc = `https://maps.google.com/maps?q=${encodeURIComponent(addressLine)}&output=embed`;
const mapTitle = `Map showing Vivid Smiles Dentistry at ${addressLine}`;
---

<section
  class:list={["section", bandModifier]}
  id={id}
  aria-label={ariaLabelText || undefined}
>
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="location-grid">
      {/* The reveal hooks sit on the two columns, NOT on the <section> — that is
          where both templates put them, and animations.js batches the copy
          column's children off `data-vs-reveal-batch` while the map fades as one
          element. Moving either up to the section would restage the whole band. */}
      <div class="location-copy" data-vs-reveal-batch>
        {eyebrowText && <span class:list={["eyebrow", eyebrowLight && "light"]}>{eyebrowText}</span>}
        {
          /* One line, `<Fragment>` inside the `<h2>` rather than indented: a
             line break inside the tag becomes a space either side of the
             heading. Same one-line form as CopyPlusStatsBlock:361.

             These three conditionals stay on SEPARATE lines and deliberately do
             not use the flush `}{` idiom. The idiom exists to stop a blank field
             adding a space inside running prose; here the three elements are
             separated by a single space in the built pages today
             (`</span> <h2>`, verified in dist/our-office/index.html), so joining
             them flush would itself be the diff. A blank field leaves one
             collapsed space between block-level children, which renders as
             nothing. */
          headingHtml && <h2><Fragment set:html={headingHtml} /></h2>
        }
        {bodyText && <p class="lede">{bodyText}</p>}

        {/* Address, phone, email and hours are site data, so the card draws
            whatever the four copy fields say — including when all four are
            blank. That is the opposite of CopyPlusStatsBlock's empty-row rule
            and it is right here: an "empty" row of this layout still carries
            the practice's address and a map, so drawing nothing would delete
            real content rather than avoid an empty band. */}
        <div class="location-card">
          <div class="row">
            <span class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><i class="fa-solid fa-location-dot"></i></span>
            <div>
              <span class="label">Address</span>
              <span class="val">{addressStreet}<br>{cityStateZip}</span>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="row">
            <span class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><i class="fa-solid fa-phone"></i></span>
            <div>
              <span class="label">Phone</span>
              <a href={phoneHref}>{phoneLabel}</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="row">
            <span class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><i class="fa-solid fa-envelope"></i></span>
            <div>
              <span class="label">Email</span>
              <a href={emailHref}>{emailAddress}</a>
            </div>
          </div>

          <div class="row">
            <span class="ic" aria-hidden="true"><i class="fa-solid fa-clock"></i></span>
            <div>
              <span class="label">Hours</span>
              <div class="hours-table">
                {officeHours.map((r) => (
                  <div class:list={["day-row", !r.opens && "closed"]}>
                    <span class="day">{r.label}</span>
                    <span class="hrs">{r.hoursLong}</span>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="location-actions">
          <Button variant={isDarkBand ? "light" : "sage"} href={directionsCta.href} hoverLabel={directionsCta.hover}>Get Directions</Button>
          <Button variant={isDarkBand ? "ghost-light" : "ghost"} href={phoneCta.href} hoverLabel={phoneCta.hover}>Call {phoneLabel}</Button>
        </div>
      </div>

      {/* Embedded Google Map — lazy-loaded so it does not block first paint.
          The aspect ratio is fixed in CSS so the band height stays stable. */}
      <div class="location-map-wrap" data-vs-reveal>
        <iframe
          class="location-map"
          src={mapEmbedSrc}
          loading="lazy"
          referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"
          title={mapTitle}
        ></iframe>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  /* ── CSS REACHABILITY, the rule this file is written against ──────────────
     Astro 6.1.9 compiles ATTRIBUTE scoping and applies the attribute to EVERY
     compound in a selector, so a two-part scoped selector lands at (0,4,0) and
     beats the page sheets' (0,2,0)/(0,3,0) rules outright — but a ONE-part
     selector, `.location-card[cid]`, lands at (0,2,0) and merely TIES
     `.contact .location-card`, leaving bundle order to decide. Nothing below is
     written as a single compound for that reason: every rule a page sheet also
     writes is nested at least one level deeper than the page's own copy of it.
     The only exceptions are the band shell rules, where both sides declare the
     identical value and no bundle order can render them differently — the same
     tie CopyPlusStatsBlock:477 lives with. */

  /* ── Band shell ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     contact.css:161 (padding), :162 (the alt background). cream, dark and sage
     complete the five-band map and are StatCalloutBlock's, unchanged.

     `background` on the base rule is this block's own: `.contact .section` sets
     none because the page body is already paper. A block that can be dropped on
     any page has to paint its own ground (R3).

     DIVERGENCE, RECORDED: our-office.css:97 uses `clamp(80px, 9vw, 120px)` for
     the same band. The block set has one rhythm — 110px/70px on all fourteen
     deployed layouts — so /our-office/ #visit adopts it and its remaining
     template-side bands keep the clamp. Vertical rhythm only; no words move.

     WHICH IS WHY THE SELECTOR IS DOUBLED, and why this block's shell differs
     from its thirteen siblings. `.section[cid]` is (0,2,0) and TIES
     `.vs-office .section` — and unlike every earlier block, the two sides do
     NOT declare the same padding here, so the tie is decided by bundle order
     and nothing else. It happens to resolve the block's way today (the built
     pages load `PageBlocks.<hash>.css` after `index_astro.<hash>.css` —
     verified in dist/general-dentistry/index.html), and that is precisely the
     kind of argument this project has agreed not to build on. `.section.section`
     compiles to `.section[cid].section` at (0,3,0), clears `.vs-office .section`
     outright, and ties nothing: no page sheet in the corpus writes padding on
     `.section.alt`. Same doubled-class trick global.css:272 uses, for the same
     reason.

     ONLY the padding is doubled. `background` stays on the single compound the
     thirteen siblings write, deliberately: at (0,3,0) it would tie
     `.vs-office .section.alt` — paper against charcoal, decided by bundle
     order — and there is no reason to buy that fight to win one this block
     does not have. At (0,2,0) the band modifier below settles the background
     on either side of the tie, and both sides say charcoal. */
  .section {
    /* Zero margin so this band cannot collapse into whichever band an editor
       drags above or below it — the rule FaqBlock.astro states and every block
       in the set follows. Inert against both page sheets today (neither writes
       a margin on `.section`, and `<section>` has no UA margin), which is why
       it can sit on the single compound. */
    margin: 0;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
  }
  .section.section {
    padding: 110px 0;
  }
  .section.cream { background: var(--vs-cream); }
  .section.dark { background: var(--vs-sage-pale); }
  .section.sage { background: var(--vs-sage); }
  .section.alt { background: var(--vs-charcoal-green); }

  /* Keep the heading clear of the global nav when the band is jumped to —
     contact.css:62-63, our-office.css:93-94, identical on both. No `--toc-h`
     term: neither page ships an "On this page" rail. */
  .section[id] {
    scroll-margin-top: calc(var(--nav-h, 77px) + 12px);
  }

  /* contact.css:39, our-office.css:44 — identical. */
  .wrap {
    max-width: 1240px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    padding: 0 40px;
  }

  /* ── The split ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     contact.css:270-273; our-office.css:326-329 is the same rule under its own
     prefix. Nested under `.wrap` to clear the tie described at the top. */
  .wrap .location-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: 80px;
    align-items: stretch;
  }

  /* ── The copy column ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     DIVERGENCE, RECORDED: our-office.css:330 adds `padding: 8px 0` here.
     contact has none, and contact is the anchor — see the header. */

  /* The page eyebrow primitive (contact.css:41-45) plus this band's own
     inline-flex rule (:274-276). Base colour is the light-band one; the dark
     bands repaint it below. Nested three deep so it clears
     `.contact .section.alt .eyebrow`-class rules on either page.

     DIVERGENCE, RECORDED: our-office.css:53-56 sizes its eyebrow 11.5px and
     spaces it with `margin-bottom: 18px` (:331); contact carries 12px and puts
     the gap in the `<h2>` margin instead. Contact's values are taken. */
  .location-grid .location-copy .eyebrow {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 12px;
    letter-spacing: 0.28em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 14px;
  }
  /* contact.css:278-280. One hairline, BEFORE only — this band's eyebrow is not
     the centred `.section-head` one, which has a rule on both sides (:190-193).
     No `content` is declared for `::after`, so no second pseudo-element is
     generated at all. */
  .location-grid .location-copy .eyebrow::before {
    content: "";
    width: 28px;
    height: 1px;
    background: var(--vs-sage);
  }

  /* contact.css:48-52 (family, weight, margin, line-height), :54 (size and
     letter-spacing), :281 (this band's margin and 14ch measure). Both pages
     compute sage for this heading on a light band (contact.css:49,
     our-office.css:78).

     DIVERGENCE, RECORDED: our-office.css:332 uses `margin-bottom: 22px` and
     carries no top margin, because its 18px sits on the eyebrow instead. */
  .location-grid .location-copy h2 {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-weight: 400;
    font-size: clamp(32px, 4.2vw, 54px);
    line-height: 1.1;
    letter-spacing: -0.022em;
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    margin: 22px 0 24px;
    max-width: 14ch;
  }
  /* `:global` because the `<em>` arrives through `set:html` and carries no scope
     attribute — a plain `h2 em` compiles to `em[cid]` and silently misses it,
     dropping the accent colour on every heading this block draws. The scope
     stays on `h2`, so it cannot leak off the block. contact.css:56. */
  .location-grid .location-copy h2 :global(em) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--vs-sage-soft);
    font-weight: 300;
  }

  /* contact.css:283-287, with the light-band colour as the base.
     DIVERGENCE, RECORDED: our-office.css:333-337 uses `line-height: 1.7`. */
  .location-grid .location-copy .lede {
    font-size: 16.5px;
    line-height: 1.75;
    max-width: 50ch;
    color: var(--vs-ink-soft);
  }

  /* ── The address card, LIGHT bands ───────────────────────────────────────
     our-office.css:341-400 — the white-card treatment, kept alive here for the
     paper, cream and sage-pale bands it was drawn for. The dark-band block
     below supersedes every colour in it. */
  .location-copy .location-card {
    margin-top: 32px;
    background: var(--vs-paper);
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    border-radius: var(--vs-r-lg);
    padding: 32px 32px 28px;
    display: grid;
    gap: 26px;
  }
  /* Geometry is identical on both sheets — contact.css:296-299,
     our-office.css:349-352. */
  .location-copy .location-card .row {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 36px 1fr;
    gap: 20px;
    align-items: start;
  }
  .location-copy .location-card .row + .row {
    padding-top: 22px;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
  }
  .location-copy .location-card .ic {
    width: 36px;
    height: 36px;
    border-radius: 50%;
    border: 1px solid var(--vs-line);
    color: var(--vs-sage);
    background: var(--vs-cream);
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    font-size: 15px;
    line-height: 1;
    margin-top: 1px;
  }
  /* our-office.css:366 / contact.css:312. FontAwesome's own rules set the glyph;
     this only stops the icon font's default line-height from off-centring it. */
  .location-copy .location-card .ic i {
    line-height: 1;
  }
  .location-copy .location-card .row > div .label {
    display: block;
    font-size: 11px;
    letter-spacing: 0.22em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--vs-muted);
    font-weight: 500;
    margin-bottom: 6px;
  }
  .location-copy .location-card .row > div .val,
  .location-copy .location-card .row > div a {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-display);
    font-size: 18.5px;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    letter-spacing: -0.005em;
    text-decoration: none;
    line-height: 1.4;
  }
  .location-copy .location-card .row > div a {
    transition: color 0.2s ease;
  }
  .location-copy .location-card .row > div a:hover {
    color: var(--vs-sage);
  }

  /* Hours table — body font, body sizing. our-office.css:381-400.
     contact.css:330-350 is the same rule with the dark-band colours, applied in
     the dark-band block below. */
  .location-card .hours-table {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-top: 4px;
  }
  .location-card .hours-table .day-row {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: baseline;
    gap: 16px;
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 15.5px;
    color: var(--vs-ink);
    letter-spacing: 0;
    font-weight: 400;
  }
  .location-card .hours-table .day-row .day {
    font-family: var(--vs-font-body);
    font-size: 14px;
    letter-spacing: 0;
    text-transform: none;
    color: var(--vs-muted);
    font-weight: 500;
  }
  .location-card .hours-table .day-row.closed .hrs {
    color: var(--vs-muted);
  }

  /* contact.css:352-355, our-office.css:402-405 — identical. */
  .location-copy .location-actions {
    margin-top: 28px;
    display: flex;
    gap: 14px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  /* ── The address card, DARK bands ────────────────────────────────────────
     contact.css:274-350 — the glass treatment, written for the charcoal band
     and applied here to `alt` and `sage`.

     THIS IS THE BLOCK THAT CHANGES /our-office/. Its sheet leaves the white card
     in place on a charcoal band; every selector here starts at `.section.<mod>`
     and so lands at (0,8,0) or better, well clear of `.vs-office
     .location-card`'s (0,4,0). See the header — the change is deliberate and
     belongs in the block-map. */
  .section.alt .location-copy .eyebrow,
  .section.sage .location-copy .eyebrow {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .location-copy .eyebrow::before,
  .section.sage .location-copy .eyebrow::before {
    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.6);
  }
  .section.alt .location-copy h2,
  .section.sage .location-copy h2 {
    color: #fff;
  }
  .section.alt .location-copy h2 :global(em),
  .section.sage .location-copy h2 :global(em) {
    color: var(--vs-eyebrow-on-sage);
  }
  .section.alt .location-copy .lede,
  .section.sage .location-copy .lede {
    color: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.86);
  }

  .section.alt .location-copy .location-card,
